talk(564,'1982-09-20','Ya. G. Sinai','P001036-P001039','Period doubling, renormalization and period tripling',r"""On P001037 Sinai proposes physics topics and then discusses Feigenbaum universality; Migdal adds further agenda items. Sinai's mathematics ends midway down P001038. The entry gives period-doubling scaling, robustness of suitable families, a suggested flow extension, complex period tripling and the renormalization fixed-point equation. Numerical approximation is distinguished from a validated proof, and the broad post-accumulation question is not treated as a uniquely defined open problem.""")
add(564,'Normalized even families do not by themselves force a cascade','P001037-P001038','definition',r"""Families satisfy \(f_\mu:[-1,1]\to[-1,1]\), \(f_\mu(-x)=f_\mu(x)\), \(f_\mu(0)=1\); \(1-\mu x^2\) is the example. The period-doubling sequence is then asserted.""",r"""A period-doubling universality theorem needs a unimodal family with nondegenerate quadratic critical point, appropriate regularity and a parameter curve crossing the renormalization stable manifold transversely. Evenness and normalization alone admit constant families and do not imply any bifurcation.""",r"""\citet[Definition of M and Theorem5]{lanford1982feigenbaum} specifies the analytic framework and verifies transverse crossing for the quadratic family. His April1981 preprint predates the talk.""",('lanford1982feigenbaum',),literal='correct with missing hypotheses',verification='read the result',reconstruction='Unimodality, quadratic criticality and parameter transversality are added; the constant-family counterexample is elementary.')
add(564,'Universal period-doubling parameter scaling','P001038','claim',r"""At successive parameters \(\mu_n\), stable periods double; \((\mu_n-\mu_{n+1})/(\mu_{n-1}-\mu_n)\to\delta^{-1}\), with \(\delta\approx4.6992\).""",r"""For an admissible transverse period-doubling family in the quadratic universality class, the scaling constant is the expanding eigenvalue of the period-doubling renormalization derivative, numerically \(\delta\approx4.669201609\). The asymptotic \(\mu_n-\mu_\infty\sim C\delta^{-n}\) requires the relevant bifurcation-locus transversality, not just existence of a fixed point.""",r"""\citet[Theorems3--5]{lanford1982feigenbaum} proves the core fixed-point and hyperbolicity assertions but explicitly notes an unproved transversality refinement after Theorem4. The entry is expository; the incorrect decimal is visible in the scan.""",('lanford1982feigenbaum',),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',reconstruction='The admissible-family qualifier and distinction between the eigenvalue theorem and full parameter asymptotic are explicit.')
add(564,'Why universality is robust for transverse families','P001038','claim',r"""The set of families with the stated bifurcation sequence is said to be open.""",r"""Near a hyperbolic renormalization fixed point with one unstable direction, its stable manifold has codimension one. A one-parameter family crossing that manifold transversely retains this local crossing under sufficiently small perturbations in the chosen differentiable topology. Infinite renormalizability itself is not an open property of individual maps.""",r"""\citet[Theorem3 and the discussion following it]{lanford1982feigenbaum} supplies the codimension-one stable manifold. The openness statement concerns families and their crossing, as the handwritten insertion \ru{сем-в} indicates.""",('lanford1982feigenbaum',),verification='read the result',reconstruction='Local family transversality replaces an incorrect reading as an open set of infinitely renormalizable maps.')
add(564,'Suggested extension from maps to differential equations','P001038','claim',r"""For \(\dot x_i=f(x_1,\ldots,x_r,\mu)\), the notes state the same scaling and an attracting periodic orbit whose basin excludes only finitely many closed trajectories. A sequence of nested loops is drawn.""",r"""Period doubling of a smooth flow is studied through a Poincare return map near a periodic orbit. To import a one-dimensional renormalization theorem one needs a specified reduction and contraction in transverse directions. The notes do not give these assumptions; a global basin assertion for arbitrary vector fields is false.""",r"""The map theorem of \citet{lanford1982feigenbaum} does not establish this general flow claim. The named ODE, phase-space region and reduction are absent, so no exact subsequent theorem is assigned.""",('lanford1982feigenbaum',),cl='F',literal='false as written',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='medium',reconstruction='A Poincare-section formulation is a proposed interpretation, not a theorem contained in the notes.',status='the unrestricted flow statement is false; its intended restricted family cannot be identified.')
add(564,'Two-parameter period tripling and complex scaling constants','P001038','claim',r"""With two parameters, period tripling can occur and a universal constant has been found.""",r"""For holomorphic quadratic-like families depending on a complex parameter, period tripling motivates a renormalization fixed point with a complex expanding eigenvalue; the real and imaginary parameters form a two-parameter family. Numerical evidence for the fixed point, spectrum and extension to nearby real two-dimensional maps is distinct from a rigorous theorem for all such families.""",r"""\citet[pp.~187--188]{golbergsinaikhanin1983tripling}, received28June1982 and published1983, is the exact speaker-coauthored match. It describes polynomial truncations and numerical eigenvalues. Its numerical evidence is not relabeled as a complete proof; the receipt date is not asserted as public circulation.""",('golbergsinaikhanin1983tripling',),cl='A',match='exact',verification='read the result',reconstruction='The complex-analytic setting and numerical character are recovered from the exact later publication.',status='the recorded numerical discovery has an exact publication. The broader real-family universality claim is not certified by that paper; no unrestricted resolution is claimed.')
add(564,'The renormalization fixed-point equation','P001038','construction',r"""\(-\alpha^{-1}f(f(\alpha x))=f(x)\) is given; the notes say renormalization arises from linearizing it.""",r"""For an even normalized unimodal map with \(a=-f(1)>0\), define \(Rf(x)=-a^{-1}f(f(-ax))\). A fixed point satisfies the notebook equation after matching the evenness and scale convention. Renormalization is the nonlinear operator \(R\); its derivative at a fixed point governs local stability and universal scaling.""",r"""\citet[Sections1--2, Theorems1 and3]{lanford1982feigenbaum} proves existence of an analytic fixed point and one-dimensional expanding spectrum. Linearization analyzes the renormalization operator; it does not define the original nonlinear map.""",('lanford1982feigenbaum',),verification='read the result',reconstruction='The scale is determined by normalization and the roles of R and its derivative are separated.')
add(564,'Degree-ten approximation and a computer-assisted proof','P001038','method',r"""The equation is said to be solved using a polynomial of degree ten and numerical computation.""",r"""A degree-ten polynomial is an approximate fixed point. A proof can combine a Newton-type map, explicit analytic tail estimates and interval arithmetic bounding rounding errors, so that a contraction theorem establishes a nearby exact analytic solution. A finite polynomial fit alone proves neither existence nor hyperbolicity.""",r"""This is precisely the method outlined in \citet[Section3]{lanford1982feigenbaum}, already circulated inApril1981. The distinction between approximation and validated bounds explains the notebook compression.""",('lanford1982feigenbaum',),verification='read the result',reconstruction='The contraction and error-control steps are supplied from the matched proof.')
add(564,'Stochastic behavior beyond period-doubling accumulation','P001038','question',r"""The question asks what happens to the vector field for \(\mu>\mu_\infty\). For maps, an ergodic invariant measure and mixing are asserted as the onset of stochasticity.""",r"""For the quadratic family there is a positive-measure set of parameters with an absolutely continuous invariant probability measure; this is not an assertion for every parameter above the accumulation point. Ergodicity alone is inadequate as a chaos criterion, since a periodic-orbit measure is ergodic. The unspecified vector-field family cannot be assigned a unique post-accumulation theorem.""",r"""\citet[Theorem2]{jakobson1980invariant} announces the positive-parameter-measure result before this talk, including robustness under \(C^3\) perturbations of families. It does not answer the unformulated flow question or eliminate periodic windows.""",('jakobson1980invariant',),cl='F',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='read the result',confidence='medium',reconstruction='The definite interval-map comparison is separated from the underdetermined question about flows.',status='the map comparison has a pre-talk theorem; no definite unresolved flow problem can be extracted.')
